-- Adaptation: this is a reduced subset of the database (the 1,024 employees with emp_no 10001 to 11000 plus every distinct manager, with all their department, title and salary rows), converted to SQLite by the DemoDB provider (https://github.com/demo-db/employees) and from SQLite to this SQL Server script by DataTug. It is shared under the same licence, Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 3.0 Unported, https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/legalcode
--
-- Requires SQL Server 2016 or later. Verified on SQL Server 2022.
-- Load it into an EMPTY database with sqlcmd, from the file (the byte order mark of this file is read as UTF-8 by -i, not by a redirect):
--   sqlcmd -S <server> -d <database> -X -x -b -f 65001 -i <file>.sql
-- -X turns off the sqlcmd commands that run programs (!! and ED), -x turns off scripting variable substitution, -b stops at the first error, -f 65001 reads UTF-8.
-- No data in this file can start a line, so a line is never a sqlcmd command; every statement is one batch ended by a GO line.
-- The script is one transaction: a failed load is rolled back and leaves the database as it was. The first statement creates the table _demodb_import and fails
-- when it exists (a second load stops there); the last one fills it, after every table was counted: a row there means the load completed.
-- DROP TABLE [_demodb_import] removes the marker once you no longer need it.
-- Text comparison in SQL Server is case-insensitive by default, and = and UNIQUE ignore trailing spaces, unlike SQLite's exact comparison:
-- keys that differ only by case or by trailing spaces in SQLite are duplicates here and stop the load. Text columns are NVARCHAR (UTF-16 code units).
-- A unique key over nullable columns allows one NULL in SQL Server and many in SQLite; a key whose columns hold several NULLs is written as a filtered unique index.
-- Tables (6): departments, employees, dept_emp, dept_manager, titles, salaries
--
-- 12 thing(s) are not carried exactly:
-- [view-not-carried] current_dept_emp: views are written in the SQL dialect of their engine and are not translated across engines
-- [key-type-bounded] departments.dept_name: a key column is carried as NVARCHAR(255) (declared "TEXT"): SQL Server cannot index an unbounded type; its longest value is 18
-- [key-type-bounded] departments.dept_no: a key column is carried as NVARCHAR(255) (declared "TEXT"): SQL Server cannot index an unbounded type; its longest value is 4
-- [type-widened] departments.dept_no: declared "TEXT" and carried as NVARCHAR(4), but it joins the columns of its foreign keys, which SQL Server requires to have one type: carried as NVARCHAR(255)
-- [key-type-bounded] dept_emp.dept_no: a key column is carried as NVARCHAR(255) (declared "TEXT"): SQL Server cannot index an unbounded type; its longest value is 4
-- [type-widened] dept_emp.dept_no: declared "TEXT" and carried as NVARCHAR(4), but it joins the columns of its foreign keys, which SQL Server requires to have one type: carried as NVARCHAR(255)
-- [view-not-carried] dept_emp_latest_date: views are written in the SQL dialect of their engine and are not translated across engines
-- [key-type-bounded] dept_manager.dept_no: a key column is carried as NVARCHAR(255) (declared "TEXT"): SQL Server cannot index an unbounded type; its longest value is 4
-- [type-widened] dept_manager.dept_no: declared "TEXT" and carried as NVARCHAR(4), but it joins the columns of its foreign keys, which SQL Server requires to have one type: carried as NVARCHAR(255)
-- [key-type-bounded] salaries.from_date: a key column is carried as NVARCHAR(255) (declared "TEXT"): SQL Server cannot index an unbounded type; its longest value is 10
-- [key-type-bounded] titles.from_date: a key column is carried as NVARCHAR(10) (declared "TEXT"): SQL Server cannot index an unbounded type; its longest value is 10
-- [key-type-bounded] titles.title: a key column is carried as NVARCHAR(18) (declared "TEXT"): SQL Server cannot index an unbounded type; its longest value is 18

